✅ 16. Synchronize a Method

16.1 Definition
🔴 A synchronized method ensures that only one thread at a time can execute that method for a particular object instance.

When a thread enters a synchronized instance method:

🔴 It acquires the monitor lock (intrinsic lock) of the current object (this).
🔴 Other threads trying to enter any synchronized instance method on the same object must wait.

16.2 Why Do We Need Synchronized Methods?

Imagine a bank account.

Initial balance:
1000

Two threads execute simultaneously:
Thread-A → Withdraw 500
Thread-B → Withdraw 700

Without synchronization:
Both threads may read the balance as 1000 before either updates it.

Possible incorrect result:
Final Balance = 500
or
Final Balance = 300

depending on timing.
This is a Race Condition.

public synchronized void withdraw(int amount) {
    ...
}
ensures only one thread updates the balance at a time.

16.3 How It Works Internally

Many people think synchronized is a keyword that magically makes code thread-safe.
Internally, the JVM treats

public synchronized void print() {

}

approximately as:

public void print() {
    synchronized(this) {
        // Method Body
    }
}

Interview Point:
A synchronized instance method locks this.

16.4 Step-by-Step Working

Suppose
count = 0

Step 1

Thread-A executes
counter.increment();

Thread-A acquires
counter monitor

Step 2

Thread-B also calls
counter.increment();

Question:

Can it enter?
❌ No.

State : BLOCKED
because Thread-A owns the monitor.

Step 3

Thread-A executes
count++;

Result
count = 1

Step 4

Thread-A exits the synchronized method.
Monitor released.

Step 5

Scheduler chooses Thread-B.
Thread-B acquires monitor.

Executes
count++;

Result
count = 2

16.5. Internal Flow

Thread-A

Acquire Monitor
↓
Execute Method
↓
Release Monitor


Thread-B

Wants Monitor
↓
BLOCKED
↓
Monitor Available
↓
Acquire Monitor
↓
Execute

